{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"The full main tier caught a REAL defect in the operation-linked trigger — and this one was mine\nby design, not by accident: 12 failed + 5 errors, scattered across unrelated interface tests,\nall \"git commit failed\" / \"git operation failed\".\n\nCause: `compact.trigger` defaults to `async`, so every close in the suite spawned a DETACHED\ncompaction worker against that test's temp store. The children outlived the tests that spawned\nthem and raced the NEXT test's writes for the store lock and the git-op lock. The workers were\ndoing legitimate work; it simply was not that process's work.\n\nTwo fixes, because there are two distinct problems and only one of them is about tests.\n\n1. PRODUCTION — the worker now STANDS ASIDE when a foreground writer holds the store lock.\n   Compaction is optional housekeeping and must yield to work someone is waiting on, never\n   compete with it. What is satisfying here: this is the bug-7084 probe, the one I proved\n   USELESS earlier in this very investigation. Inline on the close path it could never fire,\n   because the closing process had released the lock microseconds before its own probe, so the\n   store always read \"free\" — that was a key part of the root-cause argument for the original\n   P0. Out of band the same probe measures something real: another SESSION's activity, because\n   the compactor is now a different process from every writer it might obstruct. Moving the\n   work is what made the guard meaningful. Racy and fail-open by design — a skipped sweep is a\n   no-op for correctness, so guessing wrong in either direction costs nothing.\n\n2. TEST HYGIENE — an autouse conftest fixture defaults `REBAR_COMPACT_TRIGGER=off` for the\n   offline suite, in the same shape as the existing network and repo-commit guards: the ambient\n   default is the safe one, and a test that exercises the trigger opts in explicitly. A unit\n   suite must not have background writers. `tests/unit/test_compact_trigger.py` opts back in to\n   `async` for itself.\n\nNote the fixture-ordering trap I walked into on the way: my module-level opt-in was written as\n\"set async if unset\", which never fired, because the conftest fixture had already put `off` in\nthe environment. Fixed to set it unconditionally; a test wanting another mode calls setenv in\nits own body, which runs after every fixture and wins.\n\nAdded a stand-aside test AND its positive control (same setup, lock not held, fold happens) —\nwithout the control the stand-aside test would pass even if the sweep were broken outright."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786616893486465001,"uuid":"4e3acd31-f16e-4d55-bf90-b957d8f7b5ae"}